  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 340 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nModerate to heavy rains have been spreading northeast across parts \nof central and northeast SD early this morning as a sfc low pressure \nsystem shifts out of the Colorado Front Range into Nebraska. \nSouthwest flow aloft remains present across the region with an \nembedded shortwave trough tracking northeast across SD/NE per water \nvapor imagery. The sfc low is expected to shift east across the \nSD/NE border area through the morning into the afternoon and then on \ninto western IA late this afternoon and evening.\n\nThe main focus in the next 12 hours or so will be the ongoing \nprecipitation falling across our forecast area. Model guidance \ncontinues to remain in fair agreement in keeping the heaviest bands \nof rain across parts of south central SD north and east into \nsections of east central and southeast SD today. Still anticipate a \n1-2 inch type rainfall to occur along and east-southeast of a line \nfrom the Pierre area toward the Redfield area and then east toward \nthe Watertown area. Lesser amounts, generally around an inch or \nless, will be found north and west of this line across the ND/SD \nborder area back into the north central portion of SD. Ensemble mean \nPWAT values of 1.75 inches remain concentrated across those \naforementioned areas and points south and east. These higher values \nremain near the maximum climatological percentiles for this time of \nyear. As the sfc low tracks south of our area today, a tighter \npressure gradient develops between it and high pressure across \nOntario leading to an east to southeast breeze gusting up over 30 \nmph at times from north central into northeast SD. With the rain and \nthick cloud cover in place today, temperatures will remain steady in \nthe upper 50s to mid 60s east. With the rain ending by midday across \ncentral SD, afternoon readings will make the mid 60s to low 70s.\n\nThe rainfall will come to an end from west to east today. Central SD \nwill see the rain taper off by mid morning. The James Valley region \nwill hold onto the showers longer through about late morning and \nmidday. Any lingering showers this afternoon will likely be confined \nto our far eastern zones. Drier conditions will take hold tonight \nand persist through most of Saturday. Cloudy skies will persist \nhowever so temperatures are expected to fall short of climatological \nnormals with daytime reading likely remaining in the 60s. The break \nfrom the rain will be brief as guidance wants to bring in the next \nupper level disturbance on southwest flow aloft by the end of the \nweekend into Monday. Rain chances with this event currently sit at a \n40-70 percent chance with the highest probabilities of precip across \ncentral and parts of northeast SD. After another weak disturbance \nbrings some showers to to parts of the area around the middle of the \nweek, shortwave ridging is progged to build in the latter half of \nnext week. This should bring our temperature trends back closer in \nline with late September normals.",
